#
# Conformance case: transaction/concurrent-non-serializable.
#
# A logic:ConcurrentComposition runs two two-step legs whose writes conflict in
# OPPOSING order (the classic write-skew / lost-update shape): the left leg
# writes situation A then B, the right leg writes B then A. Under the
# deterministic index-order interleaving the engine DERIVES the
# logic:ConcurrentHistory and its logic:precedes conflict edges — left-step-0
# (writes A) precedes right (their shared A conflict is left-before-right), while
# left-step-1 (writes B) is preceded BY right (their shared B conflict is
# right-before-left). Those two edges form a two-transaction cycle, so the reused
# detector raises a logic:SerializationAnomaly carrying the logic:anomalyCycle —
# a history-level finding, NEVER a contradiction witness and never silently
# linearized. This is the non-serializable half of the conflict-serializability
# contract.
#
# CRITICAL: the teleology evaluator seeds ONLY from input.nq world facts; this
# input.logic.ttl carries the minimal, clean-compiling, certifiable program axiom
# the projections need (no asserted teleology triples here — the world facts live
# in input.nq, exactly as the foundation conformance cases keep theirs).

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://example.org/teleology/concurrent-non-serializable/> .

ex:GoalExpression a logic:GoalExpression .

# The compile-time reasoning contract selects the Evolution facet that classifies this
# transaction program. logic:TransactionPathEvolution drives certification to the
# transaction-path decidability class (conflict-serializability; PTIME classification,
# not search).
ex:TransactionContract a logic:ReasoningContract ;
    logic:evolution logic:TransactionPathEvolution .
